# DSH plugin-install probe — the TARBALL install route, measured end to end
#
#   node deepblend/tools/dsh-plugin-install-probe.mjs --spec 'https://github.com/pearjelly/deep-blend/releases/latest/download/deepblend-bundle.tgz'
#
# Run with an EMPTY `npm_config_store_dir` (see the tarball log for why) and with
# `npm_config_registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/`, because this machine's configured registry is
# a mirror and a mirror does not carry a package published minutes ago.
#
# Taken 2026-09-23, minutes after the 0.2.2 release, with the same criteria applied to all three
# routes — that sameness is the point of `--spec`, and the criteria are:
#
#   installed version   the bundle the profile actually got, and the six exact pins it carries
#   workbench route     `GET /deepblend/workbench` — the route ONLY the current version has, and
#                       the reason a version number alone is not enough: a stale artifact states
#                       its own version truthfully, while a route it does not have answers 404
#   presets             both deployed, byte-identical to `deepblend/presets/`, `problem: null`
#
# What it is evidence for: the third route, the one that needs no registry at all. The
# distinguishing reading is `packages pnpm fetched: 1` — the artifact carries its six siblings
# inside its own `node_modules` through npm's bundledDependencies and resolves nothing.
#
# THE STORE LINE IS NOT DECORATION, and this route is the only one where it changes the answer.
# The asset name is VERSION-FREE on purpose (`/releases/latest/download/` takes the filename
# literally, so a versioned name dies at the next release) — and the consequence is that the URL
# is BYTE-IDENTICAL across releases. pnpm's store is keyed by that URL, so on a machine that
# installed the previous release the same command installs the PREVIOUS ARTIFACT, into a brand-new
# $DSH_HOME, with no warning from anyone. MEASURED on this very release: with the shared store the
# reading was `installed version: @deepblend/dsh-blender-bundle@0.1.0` and
# `workbench route: HTTP 404`; `curl` on that same URL followed a 302 to `…/download/v0.2.0/…`
# and served the new bytes. The store above is an empty directory, which is how the question
# about the CURRENT release is asked.
#
# Body is the tool's output.
